1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,242
Imagine a machine that can create money
out of thin air.

2
00:00:04,342 --> 00:00:07,669
Not counterfeit money. Real, brand-new
dollars.

3
00:00:07,969 --> 00:00:10,790
Now, if a million of those dollars came
out tomorrow,

4
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:12,742
would it matter who got them first?

5
00:00:13,392 --> 00:00:15,526
It turns out, it matters a lot.

6
00:00:15,576 --> 00:00:22,824
It may be why your rent keeps climbing,
why your savings seem to shrink, and why
the people at the top keep pulling further
ahead.

7
00:00:23,024 --> 00:00:24,986
It's called the Cantillon Effect.

8
00:00:26,236 --> 00:00:33,260
Three hundred years ago in Paris, a banker
named Richard Cantillon had a front-row
seat to a wild experiment.

9
00:00:33,410 --> 00:00:36,797
France had started printing paper money
like never before.

10
00:00:36,847 --> 00:00:40,992
Cantillon made a fortune, and he noticed
something most people missed.

11
00:00:41,142 --> 00:00:44,180
New money doesn't spread through an
economy evenly.

12
00:00:44,230 --> 00:00:47,784
It enters at one specific point, and
ripples outward.

13
00:00:48,734 --> 00:00:51,181
Picture a drop falling into a still pond.

14
00:00:51,431 --> 00:00:54,130
The water closest to the splash moves
first.

15
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:55,841
The far shore feels it last.

16
00:00:56,091 --> 00:00:58,610
With money, the ripple is rising prices.

17
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:01,673
And where you stand in that pond changes
everything.

18
00:01:02,623 --> 00:01:07,061
Let's shrink it down to a tiny town, where
a loaf of bread costs one dollar.

19
00:01:07,311 --> 00:01:12,423
One day, a stack of new money is created,
and handed to one person: the banker.

20
00:01:12,473 --> 00:01:13,736
He's first in line.

21
00:01:13,936 --> 00:01:19,580
He spends it right away, hiring builders
and buying property, all at yesterday's
prices.

22
00:01:19,780 --> 00:01:22,323
The builder he hires gets that money next.

23
00:01:22,373 --> 00:01:26,343
With more money chasing the same goods,
prices start to creep up.

24
00:01:26,393 --> 00:01:29,510
But her pay went up first, so she barely
notices.

25
00:01:29,710 --> 00:01:31,911
Then the money reaches the shopkeeper.

26
00:01:31,961 --> 00:01:35,488
Business is booming, so bread now costs a
dollar fifty.

27
00:01:35,688 --> 00:01:37,619
And the teacher and the retiree?

28
00:01:37,669 --> 00:01:39,890
Their incomes haven't changed at all.

29
00:01:39,940 --> 00:01:41,779
But bread now costs two dollars.

30
00:01:42,129 --> 00:01:45,090
Nobody stole anything. Nobody broke the
law.

31
00:01:45,340 --> 00:01:50,684
And yet, wealth quietly moved, from the
people at the back of the line, to the
people at the front.

32
00:01:51,634 --> 00:01:53,818
Now, scale that up to today's world.

33
00:01:53,968 --> 00:01:59,678
Modern money is created by central banks,
and by commercial banks every time they
make a loan.

34
00:01:59,878 --> 00:02:09,517
The first to touch it are banks,
governments, and big investors, who can
borrow cheaply and buy assets, like stocks
and real estate, before prices move.

35
00:02:09,717 --> 00:02:12,133
So asset prices tend to rise first.

36
00:02:12,183 --> 00:02:16,719
Then the money trickles into businesses,
and eventually, into wages.

37
00:02:16,769 --> 00:02:19,012
Wages are almost always last.

38
00:02:19,362 --> 00:02:22,977
In twenty twenty, trillions of new dollars
were created.

39
00:02:23,027 --> 00:02:26,074
Within months, the stock market hit record
highs.

40
00:02:26,224 --> 00:02:29,374
A year later, groceries and rent started
to climb.

41
00:02:29,424 --> 00:02:32,805
And wages? For two years, they fell behind
prices.

42
00:02:33,755 --> 00:02:35,493
So what does this mean for you?

43
00:02:35,743 --> 00:02:39,412
If you earn a paycheck, you're usually
near the back of the line.

44
00:02:39,462 --> 00:02:43,131
Your raise tends to arrive after prices
have already moved.

45
00:02:43,381 --> 00:02:46,537
If you save in cash, its value slowly
leaks away.

46
00:02:46,587 --> 00:02:49,818
It's like walking up an escalator that's
going down.

47
00:02:50,068 --> 00:02:54,759
And if you don't own a home yet, it can
run away from you faster than you can save
for it.

48
00:02:55,009 --> 00:03:02,196
Meanwhile, people who already own assets,
and who sit closest to the source, get
richer without doing anything differently.

49
00:03:02,296 --> 00:03:03,865
That's how the gap grows.

50
00:03:04,715 --> 00:03:07,370
The Cantillon Effect isn't a conspiracy.

51
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:09,330
It's simply how new money moves.

52
00:03:09,380 --> 00:03:12,901
But because it's invisible, most people
never notice it.

53
00:03:13,151 --> 00:03:15,694
Inflation isn't just prices going up.

54
00:03:15,744 --> 00:03:20,131
It's a transfer, from the people at the
back of the line, to the people at the
front.

55
00:03:20,481 --> 00:03:23,469
Once you see that, you start asking better
questions.

56
00:03:23,519 --> 00:03:26,257
Where is new money going? Who benefits
first?

57
00:03:26,307 --> 00:03:28,562
And what am I storing my hard work in?

58
00:03:29,312 --> 00:03:34,040
So the next time you hear that new money
is being created, don't just ask how much.

59
00:03:34,290 --> 00:03:35,909
Ask: who gets it first?
